      "final_message": "I rewrote `semver_compare` in `solution.py`, and both public tests pass (`python3 -m unittest -v test_public.py`: 2 tests, OK).\n\n- **Parsing:** each argument must be exactly `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`. A number can't have leading zeros, so `\"0\"` is allowed but `\"01\"` is not. Only ASCII digits 0\u20139 count. The whole string has to match, with no extra characters around it.\n- **Errors:** a string that isn't a valid version raises `ValueError`, and a non-string argument raises `TypeError`.\n- **Comparison:** versions are compared as number tuples, returning -1, 0 or 1. That makes `1.10.0` greater than `1.9.9`, which a plain string comparison would get wrong.\n\nI also checked some extra cases by hand. It rejects missing parts, a trailing newline, pre-release tags like `-a`, non-ASCII digits, leading zeros in any part, empty strings and surrounding spaces. It raises `TypeError` for a number.\n\nDONE: Rewrote semver_compare to parse strict MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH strings (TypeError for non-strings, ValueError for invalid strings) and compare them as tuples, returning -1/0/1.",
